-- Illustrative rules, not a statute, for where L4 raises an error at an input
-- the witness gate would test (smucclaw/l4-ide#1096).
--
-- A share divides a hundred by the difference of two inputs, x and y, when
-- either of them is five. At five and five the difference is zero, so L4
-- raises there, and the combination in which both conditions hold cannot be
-- tested. The gate falls back to testing the condition as written, and the
-- note in the emitted document says why, quoting the error and naming the
-- input. The gate tests the first eight inputs that make that condition
-- true. z, which no condition reads, is tried at four values and varies
-- first, so those eight are the four with x five and y zero and the four
-- with x zero and y five. L4 answers at each of them, and the gate is built.
@export default A share that divides by a difference
GIVEN z IS A NUMBER, x IS A NUMBER, y IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
DECIDE `share` z x y IS
    IF x EQUALS 5 OR y EQUALS 5
    THEN 100 DIVIDED BY (x MINUS y)
    ELSE 0

-- The reciprocal of zero raises at the only input where its one condition
-- holds, so no gate can be built, and the message on stderr quotes the error.
@export The reciprocal of zero, which has none
GIVEN n IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
DECIDE `reciprocal at zero` n IS
    IF n EQUALS 0
    THEN 1 DIVIDED BY n
    ELSE 0
